<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    ============================================================
    bg_bubble_sent — background for sent (own) message bubbles
    ------------------------------------------------------------
    Referenced by:
      - item_message_sent.xml (root container background)

    Constraints this file honors:

    1. THEME ATTRIBUTE FILL, NOT COLOR RESOURCE.
       The <solid> uses "?attr/pulseBubbleSent", which resolves
       through the active theme:
         - light theme → @color/bubble_sent_light (#6D5EF5)
         - dark theme  → @color/bubble_sent_dark (#4E42D1)
       A single drawable file therefore serves both themes,
       with no drawable-night/ duplicate required. Do NOT
       change to a hardcoded @color/bubble_sent_light —
       that would pin the bubble to the light-theme indigo
       and it would render incorrectly in dark mode.

    2. ROUNDED RECTANGLE WITH DIRECTIONAL TAIL CORNER.
       Three corners use the standard @dimen/bubble_radius
       (18dp). The bottom-right corner uses
       @dimen/bubble_tail_radius (4dp) to hint at the tail
       direction — the same visual language used by every
       messaging app. The 4dp corner is not a literal tail
       triangle; it is a "subtle asymmetry" that conveys
       direction without the visual noise of an explicit
       triangular tail.

       Why the bottom-RIGHT (not bottom-left):
         Sent bubbles are right-aligned in the chat. The
         tail corner faces outward — toward the screen edge
         on the same side as the bubble. This mirrors the
         received bubble's tail direction (bottom-left,
         toward the incoming side). The result: bubbles
         visually "point" toward their sender, which is
         what every messaging UI does.

    3. WHY NOT A LITERAL TAIL TRIANGLE.
       Some messaging apps (older WhatsApp, iMessage in
       some versions) draw an explicit triangular tail on
       the bubble. At 24dp-to-300dp bubble widths, the
       triangle either dominates the bubble (too big) or
       disappears (too small). The modern Material
       messaging pattern uses a rounded asymmetric corner
       instead — it reads as "directional" without
       requiring a triangle that is hard to size
       correctly across bubble widths.

       Do NOT add a triangular tail to this drawable.
       If the design team insists on a literal tail later,
       it must be implemented as a separate vector that
       is layered behind the bubble shape (not as part of
       the <shape> drawable, which cannot express a
       triangle + rounded rect as one primitive).

    4. NO STROKE, NO GRADIENT, NO SHADOW.
       The bubble is flat-filled. Elevation/depth is
       conveyed by the contrast between the bubble color
       and the chat background, not by an inner shadow or
       outer stroke. This matches the flat Material 3
       aesthetic and keeps the bubble performant.

    5. NO android:tint ON THE DRAWABLE.
       Same discipline as every shape drawable in the
       project: the fill is set via ?attr/pulseBubbleSent
       (which resolves through the theme), NOT via a
       runtime setBackgroundTintList call. Adding a tint
       would layer on top of the themed color, producing
       a slightly different hue than the theme intends.

    No <size> element is declared. Shape drawables are
    sized by their consumer View's bounds — the bubble's
    width is determined by the message content (max-width
    constrained to bubble_max_width_percent), and its
    height by the number of text lines. Adding <size> here
    would set intrinsic dimensions that the view would
    then have to override, producing inconsistent bubble
    widths. The correct pattern for shape drawables that
    must size to content is to omit <size> entirely.
    ============================================================
-->
<shape
    xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:shape="rectangle">

    <solid android:color="?attr/pulseBubbleSent" />

    <corners
        android:topLeftRadius="@dimen/bubble_radius"
        android:topRightRadius="@dimen/bubble_radius"
        android:bottomLeftRadius="@dimen/bubble_radius"
        android:bottomRightRadius="@dimen/bubble_tail_radius" />

</shape>
